If $g$ is a standard Gaussian vector in $\mathbb R^n$, then $$\label{ext:dual-formula}
 \sqrt C=
 \max_{\sum_{i=1}^N\|v_i\|^2=1}
       \mathbb E\max_{1\le i\le N}\langle v_i,g\rangle.$$ The supremum defining $C$ is attained. Moreover, for every $1\le k\le N$ and every list $v_1,\ldots,v_k\in\mathbb R^n$, $$\label{ext:shorter-list}
 \mathbb E\max_{1\le i\le k}\langle v_i,g\rangle
 \le \sqrt C\left(\sum_{i=1}^k\|v_i\|^2\right)^{1/2}.$$

Set $$A=\begin{cases}0.929,&m=5,\\0.884,&m\ge6.\end{cases}$$ For every $i$, $$\label{sc:three-bounds}
 r_i\le\frac23,\qquad P_i\ge Ar_i^2,\qquad
 P_i\ge0.415r_i+0.15r_i^2.$$

Let $d,k$ be positive integers, let $g$ be standard Gaussian in $\mathbb R^d$, and let $v_1,\ldots,v_k\in\mathbb R^d$, with $\bar v=k^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^k v_i$. Then $$\mathbb E\max_{1\le i\le k}\langle v_i,g\rangle
 \le\frac{3}{2\sqrt{2\pi}}
       \left(\sum_{i=1}^k\|v_i-\bar v\|^2\right)^{1/2}.$$ The constant is sharp already for three vectors in the plane.
